course: biology
lesson: 166
title: Australia's venomous animals and why few people die
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Australia has some of the most venomous snakes, spiders, jellyfish and sea creatures in the world, yet only a handful of people die from them in a normal year.
explain: The reasons are simple: most of these animals are shy and bite only when grabbed or stepped on, antivenoms (medicines that undo venom) exist for the worst ones, and people learn rules like checking under the outdoor toilet seat and swimming inside stinger nets.
explain: Think of a dog that growls behind a fence: it is dangerous only if you climb in, and venomous animals are the same, so leaving them alone keeps you safe.
explain: Venom is something an animal injects with a bite, sting or spine; some animals only have irritating hairs, and a horse or a cow hurts more Australians every year than all the snakes and spiders together.
fact: The inland taipan has the most toxic venom of any land snake when tested on mice in the laboratory, but it lives in remote desert, is shy and has almost never bitten anyone.
fact: The eastern brown snake causes more serious bites because it lives near farms and towns where it meets people.
fact: The box jellyfish has stinging cells on long tentacles whose venom can stop a person's heart, so tropical beaches put up stinger nets in summer.
fact: Vinegar stops the unfired stinging cells of a box jellyfish from firing, so lifeguards carry it, and anyone stung needs urgent medical help.
fact: The Sydney funnel-web spider is one of the most dangerous spiders, and males wander in summer looking for mates; since an antivenom was made in 1981 no one is known to have died from a funnel-web bite.
fact: The blue-ringed octopus is small enough to sit on a hand but carries venom that can stop breathing, and its blue rings flash only when it is alarmed, as a warning.
fact: The stonefish looks exactly like a lumpy stone and has venomous spines along its back, so reef shoes in shallow water protect your feet.
fact: Cone snails fire a harpoon-like tooth full of venom into fish, and scientists have made a strong painkiller from one cone snail venom.
fact: Male platypuses have a venomous spur on each back ankle; the venom does not kill people but can cause pain that lasts for weeks.
fact: Some caterpillars have hairs or spines that sting or cause rashes, so not every animal that hurts is venomous.
fact: The redback spider is a cousin of the black widow and hides in dry sheltered places, which is why Australians check under outdoor toilet seats and garden chairs.
fact: Sea snakes are very venomous but usually gentle and rarely bite people, and their tails are flat like oars for swimming.
fact: More Australians are hurt by horses, cows and bees than by snakes or spiders.
exercise: Which land snake has the most toxic venom when tested on mice? | The inland taipan | The eastern brown snake | The sea snake | The python
exercise: Why has the inland taipan almost never bitten anyone? | It is shy and lives in remote desert | It has no fangs, so it cannot bite | It only ever bites other snakes in its burrow | It sleeps underground all year round
exercise: Why does the eastern brown snake cause more bites than the inland taipan? | It lives near farms and towns | Its venom is many times stronger than the taipan's | It chases people on purpose when it sees them | It is the only snake that lives in Australia
exercise: What makes a box jellyfish dangerous? | Stinging cells on long tentacles, with venom that can stop a heart | Sharp teeth all around its mouth that bite through skin | It wraps around small boats and pulls them under | It squirts ink into swimmers' eyes
exercise: What is vinegar used for on a box jellyfish sting? | To stop unfired stinging cells from firing | To cure the whole sting completely at once | To wash off the smell so sharks do not come | To make the jellyfish let go and swim away
exercise: Why do tropical beaches put up stinger nets? | To keep box jellyfish away from swimmers | To catch fish for the lifeguards' lunch | To stop sharks only, since jellyfish are harmless | To mark where the sand ends and the sea begins
exercise: Since 1981, why has no one died of a funnel-web bite? | An antivenom was made | All funnel-webs died out | They stopped biting | People wear spider-proof clothes
exercise: When do male funnel-web spiders wander about? | In summer, looking for mates | In winter, looking for warm beds | Only at noon | Never, they stay in their burrows
exercise: When do the blue rings of a blue-ringed octopus flash? | When it is alarmed, as a warning | All the time, just to look pretty in the rockpool | Only when it is fast asleep | Only when it is hungry and hunting crabs
exercise: How can you protect your feet from stonefish? | Wear reef shoes in shallow water | Walk backwards so it can see you coming | Clap your hands in the water to scare it off | Carry a torch even in the middle of the day
exercise: How does a cone snail catch a fish? | It fires a harpoon-like tooth full of venom | It squeezes it to death between the two halves of its shell | It traps it with sticky slime | It chases it at high speed across the reef
exercise: What useful thing came from cone snail venom? | A strong painkiller | A sun cream for the beach | A strong glue for mending shoes | A cure for the common cold
exercise: Where is a male platypus's venom? | In a spur on each back ankle | In a pouch inside its duck-like bill | In the tip of its flat tail | In its tiny teeth
exercise: What does a platypus sting do to a person? | Causes pain that can last for weeks | Kills them within a minute, like a cobra | Nothing at all, it is only a tickle | Turns the skin blue for ever
exercise: Why can a hairy caterpillar hurt you? | Its hairs or spines can sting or cause rashes | It bites with a pair of venom fangs like a spider | It squirts acid at your eyes | It wraps around your finger and squeezes it
exercise: Why do Australians check under outdoor toilet seats? | Redbacks hide in dry, sheltered places like that | Snakes live inside all outdoor toilets in Australia | Frogs jump out of them at night | Platypuses swim up the pipes into them
exercise: How do sea snakes usually behave towards people? | They are gentle and rarely bite | They attack every swimmer they see in the water | They chase boats far out to sea | They bite only children, never grown-ups
exercise: Which animals hurt more Australians than snakes or spiders? | Horses, cows and bees | Kangaroos and koalas, which attack hikers | Sharks and whales | Penguins and seals that bite people on beaches
test: «Australia's got the deadliest animals in the world, everyone dies there» What is right? | It has many very venomous animals, but very few people die of them | Yes, hundreds of people die from bites and stings every single day | Yes, nobody can go outside | No, Australia has no venomous animals at all, that's a myth
test: «found a little octopus with blue rings in a rockpool, gonna pick it up for a pic» What is right? | No, leave it, its venom can stop a person breathing | Yes, the blue rings mean it is friendly and wants to play | Yes, small octopuses are always harmless | No, it will only squirt ink on your phone
test: «picked up a pretty shell on the reef, cone shaped, nice» What should you do? | Put it down carefully, a live cone snail can sting | Keep it in your pocket and take it home | Hold it to your ear to hear the sea inside it | Squeeze it hard to see what is living inside
test: In a cartoon a spider is a cuddly friend who spins words in a web to save a pig. What is wise with a real redback? | Leave it alone and check sheltered spots before sitting | Pick it up gently and pet it, it is shy | Let it crawl on your hand, it only bites flies | Feed it crumbs every day so it stays friendly
test: What would happen if no antivenom for funnel-webs existed? | Some bites might be deadly again, as before 1981 | Nothing, because the bite was never dangerous anyway | Funnel-webs would just stop biting people | Everyone would be safe anyway
test: «a rock just moved on the reef and it's got eyes??» What is it most likely? | A stonefish, which has venomous spines | A sea turtle sleeping on the sandy bottom | A crab with a shell | A cone snail that has climbed out of its shell
test: «my mate got stung by a hairy caterpillar so it's venomous like a snake right» What is right? | Not quite, many caterpillars just have irritating hairs, not injected venom | Yes, all caterpillars have tiny fangs full of snake venom | Yes, it was a baby snake dressed as a caterpillar | No, caterpillars cannot hurt anyone ever
test: «3 redbacks, 2 funnel-webs under the shed, how many spider legs is that, gross» | 40 | 20 | 30 | 48
test: A farmer counts injuries on his farm this year. Which is most likely to have hurt someone? | A horse or a cow | An inland taipan | A blue-ringed octopus | A box jellyfish
test: «a platypus kicked me and it hurts like hell, will i die» What is right? | No, it is not deadly, but it can hurt for weeks, so see a doctor | Yes, within a minute, because platypus venom kills everyone | No, platypuses have no venom of any kind | Yes, unless you drink vinegar
test: «stinger net, ugh, why can't we just swim anywhere» What is right? | The nets keep box jellyfish away from swimmers | They stop people swimming too far out to sea | They catch crabs for dinner | They are only there for decoration and photos
test: «sea snake!! it's coming for us» What is right? | Sea snakes are venomous but usually gentle, so stay calm and let it pass | It will attack, so swim towards it first and scare it | It wants food, so feed it from your hand | Sea snakes are not venomous, so pick it up
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the inland taipan, notwithstanding the toxicity of its venom, has scarcely ever bitten a human being. | It is of a retiring disposition and inhabits remote desert regions | It is devoid of fangs and therefore entirely incapable of biting | Its venom is harmless to human beings and dangerous only to mice | It is active exclusively during the winter
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the use of vinegar in the treatment of a box jellyfish sting. | It prevents the discharge of stinging cells which have not yet fired | It neutralises the venom already injected entirely | It is applied solely to remove the odour of the sting from the skin | It is employed to drive the jellyfish away
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the mortality attributable to venomous animals in Australia? | Notwithstanding their great number, very few deaths occur in a typical year | Several hundred deaths occur each day throughout the whole of the continent | Deaths exceed those from all other causes combined | No venomous animal is to be found on the continent
exam: Ascertain the consequence for public safety of the development of the funnel-web spider antivenom in 1981. | No death from a funnel-web bite is known to have occurred since its introduction | Funnel-web spiders thereafter ceased entirely to bite human beings | The species was rendered extinct | Bites thereafter became considerably more frequently fatal than before
